# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional HTTPS front door for an internal deployment with no public domain
# (DEPLOY.md section 12 — the "https" compose profile). Not used and not
# started unless that profile is explicitly enabled: everyone else keeps
# talking to the "web" service on WEB_PORT exactly as before.
#
# "tls internal" — no external tool, no certificate file, no extra command on
# the server at all: Caddy generates its own local certificate-authority and
# leaf certificate for {$FT_HTTPS_HOST} the first time it starts, and keeps
# reusing the same one after (cached in the caddy_data volume) rather than
# regenerating it every restart. The one manual step this does not remove —
# there is no way to remove it and still be a real certificate — is telling
# OTHER computers to trust that CA once; see DEPLOY.md section 12 for the one
# command that exports it and where to install it.
#
# {$FT_HTTPS_HOST} (bare host/IP, no scheme) is read from the environment
# (.env) rather than written here, because it is different for every
# deployment (a LAN IP, a .local hostname, ...) and this file is shared,
# version-controlled source — see FT_HTTPS_HOST in .env.example. Caddy
# substitutes {$VAR} tokens anywhere in this file before parsing it, so the
# same host feeds both blocks below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
https://{$FT_HTTPS_HOST} {
	tls internal
	reverse_proxy web:8000
}

# Plain-HTTP requests to the same address redirect to the HTTPS one instead of
# falling through to Caddy's own default 404 page.
http://{$FT_HTTPS_HOST} {
	redir https://{$FT_HTTPS_HOST}{uri} permanent
}
